Task 1: Inspecting Block Devices
Subtask 1.1: List Block Devices with lsblk
Open a terminal and run:

lsblk
Output: Shows all block devices in a tree format with names, sizes, and mount points.
For more details, use:

lsblk -f
Output: Displays filesystem types and UUIDs.
Subtask 1.2: Examine Partitions with fdisk
List available disks:

sudo fdisk -l
Output: Shows partition tables for all disks.
Inspect a specific disk (replace /dev/sdX with your disk):

sudo fdisk -l /dev/sdX
Subtask 1.3: Identify Filesystems with blkid
Run:
sudo blkid
Output: Lists all block devices with UUIDs and filesystem types.
Task 2: Mounting and Unmounting Filesystems
Subtask 2.1: Mount a Filesystem Temporarily
Create a mount point:

sudo mkdir /mnt/mydisk
Mount a partition (replace /dev/sdX1 with your partition):

sudo mount /dev/sdX1 /mnt/mydisk
Verify the mount:

df -h /mnt/mydisk
Expected Outcome: The partition should appear in the output.
Subtask 2.2: Unmount a Filesystem
Unmount the partition:

sudo umount /mnt/mydisk
Verify:

df -h
Expected Outcome: The partition should no longer appear.
Task 3: Configuring Persistent Mounts (/etc/fstab)
Subtask 3.1: Add an Entry to /etc/fstab
Get the UUID of your partition:

sudo blkid /dev/sdX1
Edit /etc/fstab:

sudo nano /etc/fstab
Add this line (replace UUID and options`):

UUID=your-uuid-here /mnt/mydisk ext4 defaults 0 2
Test the configuration:

sudo mount -a
Expected Outcome: No errors. Verify with df -h.
Task 4: Managing LVM Volumes
Subtask 4.1: Create an LVM Volume
Initialize a physical volume (replace /dev/sdX):

sudo pvcreate /dev/sdX
Create a volume group:

sudo vgcreate myvg /dev/sdX
Create a logical volume:

sudo lvcreate -L 5G -n mylv myvg
Format and mount:

sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/myvg/mylv
sudo mkdir /mnt/lvm
sudo mount /dev/myvg/mylv /mnt/lvm
Subtask 4.2: Extend an LVM Volume
Extend the logical volume:

sudo lvextend -L +2G /dev/myvg/mylv
Resize the filesystem:

sudo resize2fs /dev/myvg/mylv
Verify:

df -h /mnt/lvm
Expected Outcome: The size should increase by 2GB.
Troubleshooting Tips
If mount fails, check dmesg for errors.
For LVM issues, use vgdisplay and lvdisplay to verify states.
Always back up /etc/fstab before editing.
